{"w": 595.3200073242188, "h": 809.0257568359375, "lines": [{"t": "13", "x": 0.907881431531324, "y": 0.9742087522985531, "w": 0.025861817072311773, "h": 0.017428414981790374, "c": 1, "size": 12.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Unfortunately, predation by feral cats on Rakiura Island led to a rapid decline in", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.12319506908974809, "w": 0.8259029142000445, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "kākāpō numbers. As a result, during 1980-97, the surviving population was", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.15152541916388573, "w": 0.8263606881187829, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "evacuated to three island sanctuaries: Codfish Island, Maud Island and Little", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.17970752416545568, "w": 0.8259682225956181, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Barrier Island. However, breeding success was hard to achieve. Rats were found", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.2080378742395933, "w": 0.8258159730392179, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "to be a major predator of kākāpō chicks and an insufficient number of chicks", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.23636822431373095, "w": 0.8265025826548488, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "survived to offset adult mortality. By 1995, although at least 12 chicks had been", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.26469857438786853, "w": 0.8259465898303653, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "produced on the islands, only three had survived. The kākāpō population had", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.2930660423122369, "w": 0.8267287527029422, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "dropped to 51 birds. The critical situation prompted an urgent review of kākāpō", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.32139637352567824, "w": 0.8268583442445515, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "management in New Zealand.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.3497267990426009, "w": 0.3066842527719928, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "In 1996, a new Recovery Plan was launched, together with a specialist advisory", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.3780571491167385, "w": 0.8259245469652973, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "group called the Kākāpō Scientific and Technical Advisory Committee and a", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.40638753691226864, "w": 0.8261723497786435, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "higher amount of funding. Renewed steps were taken to control predators on the", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.4347178869864063, "w": 0.8266836417232681, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "three islands. Cats were eradicated from Little Barrier Island in 1980, and possums", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.4630482747819364, "w": 0.8261859855974996, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "were eradicated from Codfish Island by 1986. However, the population did not", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.49140337008956114, "w": 0.8260120007508928, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "start to increase until rats were removed from all three islands, and the birds were", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5197337201636988, "w": 0.8267344941003553, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "more intensively managed. This involved moving the birds between islands,", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5480641079592289, "w": 0.8260332234163303, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "supplementary feeding of adults and rescuing and hand-raising any failing chicks.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5763944580333665, "w": 0.8268583442445515, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "After the first five years of the Recovery Plan, the population was on target. By", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6047248458288966, "w": 0.8258594436196312, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "2000, five new females had been produced, and the total population had grown to", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6330551959030343, "w": 0.8266268428988602, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "62 birds. For the first time, there was cautious optimism for the future of kākāpō", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6612743433120499, "w": 0.8268583442445515, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "and by June 2020, a total of 210 birds was recorded.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6896046933861876, "w": 0.5294552398094553, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Today, kākāpō management continues to be guided by the kākāpō Recovery Plan.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7179351189031102, "w": 0.8267344941003553, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Its key goals are: minimise the loss of genetic diversity in the kākāpō population,", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7462654689772478, "w": 0.8267430036715211, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "restore or maintain sufficient habitat to accommodate the expected increase in", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7745958190513854, "w": 0.8260115906510775, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "the kākāpō population, and ensure stakeholders continue to be fully engaged in", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8029261691255231, "w": 0.8267013785402764, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "the preservation of the species.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8312565191996607, "w": 0.3242210435953533, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}], "source": "pdf text layer"}